// Declarations and the compilation unit — the two types two files here have stopped on.
//
// `./genlower.wac` *"stops at a signature because a generator lowering emits a declaration"*, and
// `./asynclower.wac` had to take a `Stmt[]` and answer an `Expr` because there was no `Decl` to take.
// Twice is the argument for writing it.
//
// It is also where `./ast.wac`'s argument is sharpest. That file counts the positional-swap hazard
// across the tree and the two worst entries are both declarations:
//
// > `StructDecl` has seven fields and fifty-four arms; `Func` has seven and forty-three, including
// > `bool exported` and `bool isAsync`, which are adjacent, identical and mean opposite things.
//
// So this file is the test of the fix that argument implies. The fix works, and the first version of
// §1 said it was blocked by something else, which was wrong — §1b keeps the mistake next to the
// correction, because the way it survived is worth more than the claim was.

import { Vec } from "core";
import { Tok, Span, Ty, Expr } from "./ast.wac";
import { Stmt } from "./stmt.wac";

/** A parameter. Named because two `Tok`s and a `Ty` in a row is the hazard in miniature. */
export struct Param { Tok name; Ty type; bool isConst; }

/** A struct's field. */
export struct Field { Tok name; Ty type; Expr? init; }

/**
 * A function.
 *
 * **Seven fields, named, and `exported` and `isAsync` are no longer adjacent positions.** The shipped
 * `DeclKind.Func(t, ty, ps, body, true, tps, false)` is the call `ast.wac` quotes; here it is
 *
 *     Func { name: t, ret: ty, params: ps, body: b, exported: true, typeParams: tps, isAsync: false }
 *
 * and swapping the two booleans is a compile error rather than a program that exports nothing and
 * awaits everything. That is the whole of the fix and it costs one keystroke per field.
 */
export struct Func {
  Tok name;
  Ty ret;
  Param[] params;
  Stmt[] body;
  bool exported;
  Tok[] typeParams;
  bool isAsync;
}

/** A struct, with its methods. */
export struct StructDecl {
  Tok name;
  Field[] fields;
  Func[] methods;
  bool exported;
  Tok[] typeParams;
  Tok? extends;
}

/** An enum and its variants; a variant's payload is a `Param[]` for the same reason. */
export struct EnumDecl { Tok name; Variant[] variants; bool exported; }
export struct Variant { Tok name; Param[] payload; }

/** `union<A, B> Name;` */
export struct UnionDecl { Tok name; Ty[] members; bool exported; }

/** `type Name;` — and `ast.wac` records that this one is written backwards more often than not. */
export struct Typedef { Ty of; Tok name; bool exported; }

/** `import { a, b } from "path";` */
export struct Import { Tok[] names; Tok path; }

/** `export { a } from "path";` — a re-export, which is an import and an export at once. */
export struct ReExport { Tok[] names; Tok path; }

/** What a top-level declaration is. */
export union<Func, StructDecl, EnumDecl, UnionDecl, Typedef, Import, ReExport> DeclKind;

export struct Decl { DeclKind kind; Span at; }

/** A file. */
export struct Program { Decl[] decls; Tok path; }

// ## 1. The fix works, and reading it works too — corrected the same day
//
// **The first version of this section said a `match` could not read one, and that was wrong.**
// `../../../GRAMMAR.ebnf` has two pattern languages: the parenthesised form binds by position and can
// match by type without binding, and the **brace pattern** `Func { name, ret, .. }:` binds by field
// name. `match_arm` is `IDENT , [ arm_payload ]` and does not care whether the `IDENT` names an enum
// variant or a union member, so a union of named structs is readable as well as constructible.
// `@/packages/wacc/src/genlower.wac`'s `generatorsIn` is written with it and §2 there is the account
// of how the wrong claim survived three commits.
//
// What survives is narrower and explains the case it was generalised from:
//
// > **The brace pattern binds field names, so an arm can read its subject exactly when the subject
// > has fields.** An enum variant with a named payload has them; a union member that is a *struct*
// > has them; a union member that is itself a *union* does not — which is `@/packages/box`'s
// > `Err(is Corrupt):`, where `Corrupt` is eight members and there is nothing to name.
//
// So this file's design holds in both directions:
//
// | | construction | reading |
// |---|---|---|
// | enum of positional variants | swappable, silent | binds by position, or by name with `{ }` |
// | union of named structs | **named, checked** | **named, checked** |
//
// The remaining difference is construction, which is the whole of the swap hazard, so the trade is
// not a trade: `ast.wac`'s fifty-four `StructDecl` arms become fifty-four constructions that cannot
// be transposed, and nothing is lost at the reading end.
//
// ## 1b. What the superseded version said
//
// Replacing the enum of positional variants with a **union of named structs** removes the swap
// hazard completely: there is no order to get wrong, `exported` and `isAsync` are names, and
// `ast.wac`'s fifty-four `StructDecl` arms become fifty-four constructions that cannot be
// transposed.
//
// And then nothing can read one. A `match` over a union matches **by type**, and
// `@/packages/box/src/gunzip.wac` found that matching by type does not bind:
//
// > `binding = IDENT | "is" , type` — a payload is bound by **name** or matched by **type**, and
// > there is no form that does both. `Err(is Corrupt c):` is the obvious spelling and is not in the
// > grammar.
//
// An enum arm binds its payload — `Func(t, ty, ps, body, ex, tps, async):` gives you seven locals,
// in the wrong order if you are unlucky, which is the hazard. A union arm gives you the knowledge
// that it is a `Func` and no way to reach `name`. So the fix for the swap trades a *silent wrong
// answer* for *a compiler that will not let you write the pass at all*, which is worse, and there is
// no third option in the language today:
//
// | | construction | reading |
// |---|---|---|
// | enum of positional variants | swappable, silent | binds seven locals |
// | union of named structs | **named, checked** | *"cannot reach the fields"* — **false** |
//
// Kept because the reasoning is worth reading against §1: I took `Err(is Corrupt c):`, a real gap
// about a *nested union*, and applied it to a union of *structs*, which differ in the one property
// the brace pattern needs. Then I promoted it, twice, on the strength of how load-bearing it would
// be if true. **How important a claim would be is not evidence for it**, and it is what made this one
// harder to check rather than easier.
//
// ## 2. What `Program` turned out to need that the shipped one does not have
//
// `Program { Decl[] decls; Tok path; }` — and the shipped `Program` has no path. A `Tok` is an index
// into *a* token array and `Span` is a line and column in *a* file, so every node in the tree is
// meaningless without knowing which file it came from, and the shipped compiler carries that
// separately in the driver. Once two files are in the same pass — which is what `Import` above makes
// possible — a `Span` from one and a `Tok` from another are the same types and mean different things.
//
// That is the phantom-parameter shape at its tenth site here, and the first where the tag would
// distinguish two values of one type that a **single function** already holds at once: a resolver
// looking up an imported name has the importing `Program` and the exporting one in scope together.
//
// ## 3. What could not be written
//
// **`Field { Expr? init; }` and `Param { bool isConst; }` are two spellings of *optional*.** A field
// with no initialiser is `null`; a parameter that is not `const` is `false`. Both are *absence*, and
// the difference is that one absence has a value worth carrying and the other does not — which is
// the right distinction and is invisible: nothing about `Expr?` and `bool` says one is *there is no
// initialiser* and the other is *the `const` keyword was absent*. In a tree where every leaf is
// `Tok` or `bool`, this is the same complaint `ast.wac` makes about `i32`, one level up.
//
// **`StructDecl.extends` is `Tok?` and `UnionDecl.members` is `Ty[]`, and both are name references
// the parser cannot resolve.** The shipped compiler resolves them in a later pass against a symbol
// table; here they are stored as *the token that spelled it*, so a `StructDecl` is not self-contained
// and a pass that wants the base struct has to have the table. That is right, and what cannot be
// written is any way to say the type is unresolved — `Tok?` and a resolved `StructDecl` would be two
// types in a language with a way to phase a tree, and are one here.
//
// **Nothing says a `Program`'s `Decl[]` is in source order and two passes depend on it.** `Import`
// must precede use for a single-pass resolver, and `ReExport` must not create a cycle. Both are
// properties of the array's order, which is the *preserved* half of the list-order entry — the caller
// owns it — and the caller here is the parser, which is not the reader.
